    "problem_statement": "# gTTSError not working as expected\n\nI noticed a strange behavior when trying to use the gTTSError class. It seems like it's not behaving like a proper exception.\n\n## What happened?\n\nWhen I try to raise a gTTSError or catch it in an exception handler, it doesn't work as expected:\n\n```python\nfrom gtts import gTTS, gTTSError\n\ntry:\n    # Try to trigger an error\n    tts = gTTS(\"Hello\", lang=\"invalid-language\")\n    tts.save(\"test.mp3\")\nexcept gTTSError as e:\n    print(f\"Caught error: {e}\")\n```\n\nInstead of catching the error properly, I get a TypeError about object initialization.\n\n## What did I expect to happen?\n\nI expected gTTSError to work like a normal exception that can be raised and caught in try/except blocks, with proper error messages as described in the documentation.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nHere's a simple script that demonstrates the issue:\n\n```python\nfrom gtts import gTTS, gTTSError\n\n# Try to create a custom error\ntry:\n    error = gTTSError(\"Custom error message\")\n    raise error\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(f\"TypeError when creating gTTSError: {e}\")\n\n# Try using the library with an invalid language\ntry:\n    tts = gTTS(\"Hello\", lang=\"xyz\")  # Invalid language code\n    tts.save(\"output.mp3\")\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f\"Got unexpected error type: {type(e).__name__}\")\n    print(f\"Error message: {e}\")\n```\n\nThis produces errors about object initialization rather than the expected behavior of a proper exception class.\n\nI'm not sure what's going on here, but it seems like the gTTSError class might not be properly inheriting from Exception or something similar."
